now a poll in the sunday times today  about the future of the uk will have  unnerved unionists across the country  this morning particularly in northern  ireland  it found a majority of people in  northern ireland would like to see a  border pole or a referendum on  unification with ireland  within five years 44  didn't think this should happen within  five years 48  meanwhile think it's likely that  northern ireland will vote for  unification with ireland  within a decade and that's more than the  44  who think that's unlikely well we're  joined by the perfect person to discuss  this northern ireland's first minister  and dup leader  arlene foster good to see you this  morning  um look in terms of in terms of this  poll  this must have you worried surely  well i think first of all we have to  remember that this is an internet pool  so that always comes  out with a health warning but whatever  about that  i think it is very disappointing to see  that some nationalist parties across the  united kingdom  during a time of national crisis when  i'm focusing every  uh working r on trying to save lives  are actually trying to take this  opportunity to talk about constitutional  politics i mean we all know  how divisive a border pole would be and  for us in northern ireland what we have  to do at the moment is all come together  to fight  against covet and not be distracted by  what would be  absolutely reckless at this time we are  going to uh  talk about kovid in just a moment but in  terms of it being  divisive i mean is it really i mean the  long-standing  rule has always been hasn't it that if a  majority of people wanted a referendum  then a referendum would  happen are you dead against that now  no it's not that i'm dead against it i  can argue for united kingdom  every day of the week because of course  the arguments are rational they're  logical and they will win through and i  mean nobody's suggesting  and not even this poll is suggesting  that  we would lose in that if there was a  border pole  but of course it would be incredibly  divisive and i think most people in  northern ireland  want us to deal with what's in front of  us at present and of course what's in  front of us  is dealing with a pandemic and actually  settling down in northern ireland we  only came back into the evolution  after three years out of devolution in  january of last year  therefore and then we came into the  pandemic in march therefore we need to  get on with dealing with all of the  things that matter to everybody in their  everyday lives  and that's what i'm focused on and  that's what everybody should be focused  on  your deputy first minister michelle  o'neill from champaign of course  has tweeted to say there is an  unstoppable conversation underway  on our constitutional future we can  overcome the barriers of partition and  build a new island well  yeah we know that's that's the ultimate  goal that that shin fein  would want but is she right to say that  this conversation has started and  whatever else is going on in the world  and these huge priorities that you talk  about  this conversation still needs to be held  well i mean the conversation's been  going on for 100 years  actually we're celebrating our centenary  now this year  and when people have the conversation  and nobody fears having the conversation  because of course we've seen throughout  the pandemic just the strength of the  united kingdom  the fact that we have uh three billion  pounds from the united kingdom  government to fight coronavirus we've  had the furlough scheme  we've had the rule out of the  vaccination in a way of course you don't  see  in the republic of ireland our national  health service has proven to be so  important to all of our citizens and  then of course because we're under such  incredible pressure at the moment  in our national health service our  military can provide that backup that  we've received just last week and when i  spoke to  the defense minister uh just on friday  ben wallace he was very clear that if we  needed any further assistance we could  have that assistance so there  in a very tangible way is actually the  strength of the united kingdom  um in terms of that strength though is  isn't that or hasn't that been damaged  from your perspective  by the whole brexit arrangements as they  currently stand i mean  you are sort of economically as much in  the eu as you are in the uk aren't you  no absolutely not our biggest market by  far  is great britain it will continue to be  great britain both in terms of who we  send our services and goods to and where  we receive our services and goods from  there are of course difficulties with  the protocol difficulties which we  warned about when it was  envisaged and put through in parliament  however we're working with  our government to make sure that we  overcome  those difficulties and whether it's in  relation to vat and second-hand cars or  tariff on steel or indeed parcels coming  into northern ireland we have to work  through those difficulties and make sure  that we find a solution  do you wish i mean i know politicians  never want to  sort of have a great deal of hindsight  in a way understandably so but do you  wish you'd back teresa may's deal  no absolutely not because of course  theresa may's deal would have left us in  the eu  customs union and indeed the single  market forever if you remember  the attorney general jeffrey cox at that  time said that we would  not have any escape rip from any uh eu  customs union or indeed the single  market so  i think people are having a little bit  of revisionism  around theresa may's deal but they  should revisit it and see what it meant  for us in northern ireland and i think  they will come to the conclusion that it  certainly was not a better deal  as you say your priority at the moment  is the covert situation  and you know obviously quite quite  rightly so um  you've said that restrictions are going  to be extended until  march march the 5th i think i mean your  health minister says it's unrealistic  for those restrictions to necessarily  end at that point do you agree with him  well i think what we'll do is on the  18th of february we'll review where we  are in terms of the pandemic what the  positive case numbers are at that point  in time how is  our hospitals coping with the pandemic  what are the number  of deaths at that time and we will  review all of that because i've often  said  none of this is inevitable what we need  to have is people working with us so  that we can  overcome this dreadful virus and now  that we have the vaccine program running  out so effectively  in northern ireland that gives a lot of  hope for people in the future  so we just need to keep working together  and make sure that we get to the end of  this journey  and make sure that we get through it so  that we can rebuild  our economy and society here in northern  ireland  it's going to be a hugely difficult few  weeks ahead though isn't it because we  know  hospitals in northern ireland are  already under tremendous strain hospital  admissions we expect to peak  some point in the next eight to ten days  the number  requiring intensive care treatment is  expected to continue  to increase for more than a fortnight  now i mean  how are you what are you doing to help  ease if you can  any of the pressure on hospitals and  icus  well it's very important that the  community realizes the pressure on our  hospitals and support  our health service workers and recognize  the pressure and sacrifice that they're  making for  the community and large and in doing so  they must keep to the restrictions and  keep to in what they have been doing i  mean  there's only a tiny minority of people  and i think this is true across the  united kingdom  who are breaking uh the restrictions so  it's important that we all stick  together  but also recognize the help that we are  getting from the military who are  sending in extra resources into our  hospital  and again as i said the ben wallace said  that if we needed  any further help in relation to that he  would be willing to do that and i think  that's really good  for us in northern ireland to know that  there that there is an extra resource  there  if we need it but in terms of planning  forward you've got to look at what's  happened in the past the head of the bma  in northern ireland says hospital staff  are exhausted and demoralized and he  said this phase will stand out as one  where we could have planned better are  you talking to people like him to see  how you can change that planning moving  forward  well look i think everybody recognizes  and i noticed the health secretary was  saying this as well at the beginning of  your program  that we are dealing with a a novel virus  therefore there's no textbook and  as to how we deal with this matter we're  learning all the time  in relation to how we combat this virus  and of course we're speaking to everyone  who has a voice in relation to these  issues  so that we can plan better but let us  hope given that we have the vaccine in  place here now  that we can move forward in a more  positive way yes there will  be new variants and we'll have to cope  with that but one of the benefits  again of being in the united kingdom is  that we're part of cog uk  which is the covet 19 genomic testing  regime  and don't forget that the uk alone tests  40 percent more than anywhere else in  the uk so we're doing 40 percent of the  genomic testing across the world  and of course because of our membership  of the united kingdom  we're part of that testing regime first  minister good to talk to you this  morning thank you

